Tier 4 machine-information CSR verification: the four read-only ID CSRs (mvendorid/marchid/mimpid/mhartid) must read without trapping, misa must read its ISA encoding and silently ignore writes (WARL), and writes to the read-only 0xF1x block must raise illegal-instruction. Also pins the exact rule that decides "is this a write": rs1 being register x0, NOT rs1 holding the value zero. STANDALONE - swap this in alone, do not combine with testcodes/6,7,8,9,10.
m4_define(['m4_asm_ECALL'],  ['32'b00000000000000000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_EBREAK'], ['32'b00000000000100000000000001110011'])
m4_define(['m4_asm_MRET'],   ['32'b00110000001000000000000001110011'])
m4_asm(ADDI,  x1,  x0,  1001000)             //   0  x1 = 72 (handler address)
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x1,  1100000101)          //   4  mtvec = 72
m4_asm(ADDI,  x2,  x0,  11111111111)         //   8  x2 = 2047 (junk payload for CSR writes)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x11, x0,  1000101011)          //  12  x11 = 555 (canary)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x12, x0,  1000101011)          //  16  x12 = 555 (canary)
m4_asm(ADDI,  x13, x0,  0)                   //  20  x13 = 0 -- a NON-x0 register that HOLDS zero

   // ---- A: reads of the read-only ID CSRs must not trap ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x3,  x0,  111100010001)        //  24  x3 = mvendorid (0xF11)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x4,  x0,  111100010010)        //  28  x4 = marchid   (0xF12)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x5,  x0,  111100010011)        //  32  x5 = mimpid    (0xF13)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x6,  x0,  111100010100)        //  36  x6 = mhartid   (0xF14)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x7,  x0,  1100000001)          //  40  x7 = misa      (0x301)

   // ---- B: misa is WARL read-WRITE -- write is accepted, dropped, and must NOT trap ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x9,  x2,  1100000001)          //  44  x9 = OLD misa; writes 2047, which must be discarded
m4_asm(CSRRS, x8,  x0,  1100000001)          //  48  x8 = misa, must be unchanged

   // ---- C: write to a read-only CSR must trap ----
m4_asm(CSRRW, x11, x2,  111100010001)        //  52  TRAP (cause 2); x11 must keep its canary

   // ---- D: rs1 holds zero but is not x0, so this IS a write -- must still trap ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x12, x13, 111100010100)        //  56  TRAP (cause 2); x12 must keep its canary

   // ---- D': rs1 IS x0, so the write is suppressed and the read-only access is legal ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x10, x0,  111100010001)        //  60  x10 = mvendorid, NO trap

m4_asm(ADDI,  x14, x0,  101010)              //  64  x14 = 42, sentinel: main path ran to completion
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   //  68  halt

   // ---- shared handler: accumulate cause, count traps, skip the faulting instruction ----
m4_asm(CSRRS, x22, x0,  1101000010)          //  72  handler: x22 = mcause
m4_asm(ADD,   x20, x20, x22)                 //  76  x20 += mcause
m4_asm(ADDI,  x21, x21, 1)                   //  80  x21 += 1 (trap counter)
m4_asm(CSRRS, x23, x0,  1101000001)          //  84  x23 = mepc
m4_asm(ADDI,  x23, x23, 100)                 //  88  x23 = mepc + 4
m4_asm(CSRRW, x0,  x23, 1101000001)          //  92  mepc = mepc + 4
m4_asm(MRET)                                 //  96  -> PC = mepc
m4_asm(ADDI,  x30, x0,  1111100111)          // 100  poison (999): must NEVER execute
m4_asm(BGE,   x0,  x0,  0)                   // 104  handler halt (unreachable)
m4_asm_end()


misa encoding assumed by this test: MXL = 2'b1 in bits [31:30] (RV32) and the extension bitmap bit
8 = 'I', everything else zero -> 0x40000100. If you encode misa differently (e.g. you decide to
report 0), adjust x7/x8/x9 below to match; nothing else in the test depends on the value.

The four 0xF1x CSRs all read zero, so their VALUES prove nothing on their own -- a trapping access
would leave the same 0 in rd. What proves they are implemented is the trap count in x21. This test
is built so that every distinct Tier-4 mistake produces a different x21.

Expected Waveforms:
Reg	Value		Why
x1	00000048	72, the handler address written to mtvec
x2	000007FF	2047, the payload every CSR write in this test tries to store
x3	00000000	mvendorid -- 0 is the legal "non-commercial implementation" encoding
x4	00000000	marchid -- 0 is the legal "not implemented" encoding
x5	00000000	mimpid -- 0 is the legal "not implemented" encoding
x6	00000000	mhartid -- 0 is the REQUIRED value here, not a sentinel: at least one hart must read 0, and this core has exactly one
x7	40000100	misa -- MXL=1 (RV32) in bits 31:30, plus bit 8 ('I'). Note Zicsr is NOT representable here; misa has no bits for multi-letter Z* extensions
x8	40000100	misa AFTER writing 2047 to it -- unchanged. This is WARL-ignore, and it works because no next-state mux is keyed on 0x301, i.e. because no storage was written for it
x9	40000100	CSRRW returns the OLD CSR value to rd, so this reads misa a second time and simultaneously proves the write at 44 did not trap
x10	00000000	mvendorid read via CSRRS with rs1=x0 -- write suppressed, so a read-only CSR is legal to touch
x11	0000022B	555, canary UNCHANGED -- the CSRRW at 52 trapped, so it never wrote its rd. Without the canary this is indistinguishable from a successful read of mvendorid (which is also 0)
x12	0000022B	555, canary UNCHANGED -- the CSRRS at 56 trapped even though x13 holds zero
x13	00000000	the non-x0 register holding zero, used as rs1 at 56
x14	0000002A	42 -- sentinel proving the main path reached the end. If any Group A/B access wrongly trapped, this still gets set (the handler returns), so x14 alone is not sufficient; read it together with x21
x20	00000004	sum of mcause across all traps = 2 + 2. Both traps were illegal-instruction; any other cause shows up here as a wrong total
x21	00000002	THE load-bearing value: exactly two traps, from address 52 and address 56, and nothing else
x22	00000002	mcause from the LAST trap = 2, illegal instruction
x23	0000003C	60 -- mepc(56) + 4 from the last trap, i.e. the return address that resumed execution at instruction 60
x30	00000000	poison never written -- address 100 sits in MRET's 2-instruction shadow on both handler passes

Notes:
- Control flow: the trap at 52 squashes 56 and 60, runs the handler, and MRETs back to 56. Address
  56 then executes for real, traps again, squashes 60 and 64, runs the handler, and MRETs back to
  60. So 56 and 60 are each fetched twice and 64/68 run once. Same double-fetch shape as
  testcodes/10, just entered twice.
- x21 is the diagnostic. Trap count by failure mode:
    x21 = 2   correct
    x21 = 10  no Tier-4 work applied at all -- all five addresses still missing from
              $csr_addr_valid, so every CSR access in Groups A/B/C/D/D' traps
    x21 = 7   EITHER the four 0xF1x addresses are missing from $csr_addr_valid (24-36 and 60 trap),
              OR the CSRRS rs1=x0 write-suppression is gone so every read-only read traps. These
              two are indistinguishable from the register file alone -- check $csr_addr_valid
              first, then $csr_wr_req in the waveform at address 24
    x21 = 5   0x301 missing from $csr_addr_valid (40, 44, 48 trap)
    x21 = 3   0x301 wrongly classified read-only, so the write at 44 traps
    x21 = 1   with x12 = 0: $csr_src_zero tested the VALUE in rs1 instead of the register number,
              so the access at 56 was treated as a read and let through
    x21 = 0   the read-only-write trap term never fires (confirm with x11 = x12 = 0), or mtvec
              never took the handler address and the trap path is dead entirely
  Independent of x21: x8 = 000007FF means misa was given real storage -- a next-state mux keyed on
  0x301 that should not exist.
- Groups C and D together are the whole read-only-write story, and D is the sharper half. The Zicsr
  rule is stated in terms of "rs1=x0", the register NUMBER, not "the value written is zero". x13
  holds 0 but is register 13, so $csr_src_zero must stay low and the access must trap. An
  implementation that tested $src1_value == 0 instead of $rs1 == 5'b0 passes C and fails D, and the
  failure is visible as x12 = 00000000 with x21 = 1.
- Group D' (address 60) is the inverse guard: it must NOT trap, because CSRRS with rs1=x0 performs
  no write and therefore cannot raise the read-only-CSR exception. Getting C right by simply
  trapping on any access to 0xF1x would break this and show as x21 = 3 with x10 = 0.
- NOT covered: writes to 0xF12 and 0xF13 (only 0xF11 and 0xF14 are exercised as write targets). All
  four share the same $csr_addr[11:10] == 2'b11 term, so covering two of them covers the logic --
  but not a per-address typo in $csr_addr_valid. A single mistyped 0xF1x address shows up as one
  extra trap (x21 = 3) with that CSR's read still returning 0, so a x21 of 3 should be checked
  against BOTH that case and the misa-read-only case above before concluding which it is.
- Run this only after the Tier 4 edits are in. On the pre-Tier-4 core it produces x21 = 10 and
  x20 = 00000014, since none of the five addresses are recognised yet.
